::CW_U03_L05_GP_Q01::Passage: The writer repeats the image of an empty chair at dinner before explaining who is missing. What is the most likely purpose?{
~A. To describe furniture only.#Incorrect. The repeated chair creates meaning beyond furniture.
~B. To prove the chair is new.#Incorrect. Age or condition is not the focus.
=C. To make the reader feel absence before receiving full explanation.#Correct. Repetition builds mood and curiosity.
~D. To avoid all emotion.#Incorrect. The image creates emotion.
}
::CW_U03_L05_GP_Q02::Passage: "Of course the award went to the student who forgot the speech and bowed like royalty." What craft choice is strongest?{
=A. Sarcastic tone.#Correct. "Of course" and "bowed like royalty" create criticism.
~B. Warm admiration.#Incorrect. The wording sounds critical, not admiring.
~C. Neutral listing.#Incorrect. The sentence has attitude.
~D. Scientific explanation.#Incorrect. This is rhetorical tone, not science.
}
::CW_U03_L05_GP_Q03::Passage: Mina folds the letter and places it under the heaviest book. What reader effect does this create?{
~A. Readers learn the book's price.#Incorrect. Price is not mentioned.
~B. Readers forget the letter exists.#Incorrect. The action draws attention to the letter.
~C. Readers receive no character information.#Incorrect. The action reveals character.
=D. Readers infer avoidance through action.#Correct. The hiding action shows what Mina does not say directly.
}
::CW_U03_L05_GP_Q04::Passage: A scene begins at a locked door, then uses flashback to explain why the narrator is afraid to knock. What purpose does this structure serve?{
~A. It removes all tension.#Incorrect. The delay builds tension.
=B. It creates curiosity before giving context.#Correct. The locked door raises a question, and the flashback answers it later.
~C. It makes the setting disappear.#Incorrect. The locked door anchors the setting.
~D. It proves flashbacks are never useful.#Incorrect. This flashback has a purpose.
}
::CW_U03_L05_GP_Q05::Passage: The writer uses short sentences as a character waits for a phone call: "The clock ticked. The phone stayed dark. She did not breathe." What is the reader effect?{
~A. The short sentences explain a recipe.#Incorrect. There is no recipe.
~B. The short sentences make the scene cheerful.#Incorrect. The scene is tense.
=C. The short sentences slow attention and increase tension.#Correct. The compressed sentences focus each small moment.
~D. The short sentences remove pacing.#Incorrect. Sentence length is part of pacing.
}
